Ivanov tells Putin: "That's the fact that at the beginning of 2012 I asked you to entrust this difficult post to me, you could even say, a problematic post, for four years.
It turns out that I've worked in this post for four years and for eight months.
Ivanov took office in December 2011.
Earlier, he was prime minister and defence minister.
He's a member of Russia's Security Council and a former employee of the KGB's national security service, just like Putin.
In the late 1990s, Putin led the Federal Security Service, which is the successor to the KGB, Ivanov took the post of deputy.
When Putin came to power, he called Ivanov among the five people he trusted most.
At the end of Putin, Ivanov was considered a potential presidential nominee for the post of Russian president, as Putin's re-election for a third term would be unconstitutional.
At that time, another close alliance of Putin became president - Dmitrijs Medvedevs.
Putin became the prime minister, and after three and a half years returned as president.
