Medication by 2 pounds per day can reduce the risk of a heart attack.
Thousands of patients who have suffered a heart attack could be treated for 2 pounds per day, as official representatives recommend using them more for people and longer periods of time.
Anti-clotting drug Ticagrelor reduces the repeated risk of heart attacks in people with heart disease.
This medicine is already prescribed for 12 months after the heart attack, reducing the risk of re-excitation.
NHS-represented NICE is recommended to use this preparation 4 years after heart attack to further reduce the risk of heart attack in people with cardiovascular problems.
However, NICE currently under NHS recommends the use of this preparation 4 years after a heart attack to further reduce the cardiovascular risk.
Approximately 140 000 people in England experience a treasury every year, and a quarter of them experience a recurrent heart attack or a stroke.
Cardiac and stroke are caused by accumulating fatty substances in the walls of the heart, forming obediences.
If obstruction ruptures, it can cause a clot, blocking the blood flow to the heart and causing a heart attack.
In turn, if a thrombus detached from the vascular hay, it can go further into the circulatory and block blood access to the brain, causing a heart attack.
People who have had a heart attack are at greater risk of repetitive heart at risk.
Ticagrelor, produced by the British firm AstraZeneca and traded under the name Brilique, reduces the risk of clot formation.
The new NICE guidelines, which was published today, are recommended for use in people 90 mg Ticagrelor for 12-month and for an additional 3 years at 60 mg twice a day with aspirin.
NICE Health Technology Director Professor Kerola Longson explains: "Despite the availability of drugs to prevent repeated heart attacks, more than a quarter of people who have had a heart attack are experiencing another heart attack or a stroke, which often has severe consequences."
Fear of repetitive heart attack can have a negative impact on the quality of a person's life.
Evidence suggests that Ticaglor combined with aspirin effectively reduces the risk of recurrent heart attack and heart attack in people who have ever experienced it.
Preliminary recommending treatment with Ticagrelor, we are delighted that we are able to expand the treatment options for the thousands of people who are thus benefiting.
Taking into account limited information on the efficacy and safety of Ticagrelor, in particular the risk of haemorrhage with this medicinal product for more than three years, the draft guidelines do not recommend its use longer than this period.
