Britain's treccus cycling team twice corrects the Olympic record and wins the sprint.
Britain's trecket team on Thursday twice corrected the Olympic record and won a sprint race for the men.
Brits corrected the first Olympic record in the qualifier, but it was re-improved by New Zealanders in the first round.
In the sprint final, the British team met with New Zealand, and the Britons stripped the New Zealanders of the Olympic record title, quitting them with silver medals.
In the final, the British team beaten by 4,440 seconds, beating the New Zealanders by 0.102 seconds.
The French team beat Australia in the bronze.
It should be noted that the record has also been rectified in the women's pursuit qualifier - the British team lactured the world record, which is 4:13.260.
President who stayed in the shadow: Gustavs Zemgals - 145.
On Friday, August 12, marks the 145th anniversary of Gustavs Zemgals.
Zemgals proclaimed the state of Latvia, but unfortunately, there has been unfortunate in the shadow of the well-described first President of Latvia Jānis Čakste and the last president of the pre-war period Kārlis Ulmmanis left undeservedly.
No one of our statesman has defended democracy and the rule of law as Zemgals, write Arnolds Auziņš in a book dedicated to Gustavs Zemgals ten years ago.
In a conversation with "Delfi", Auziņš emphasizes that Zemgals is one of the few high-ranking politicians who had a sense of humour.
The rest such stiff, always very right.
He couldn't be able not to, nowadays it could be similar to what Raimonds Vējonis is like.
"He was one who is close to the people," says Auziņš.
Modern politicians do not know history badly, they invoked Kārlis Ulmanis as a good leader, but rarely anyone knows how furiously he was.
In government meetings, the only person to whom he did not shout, was General Balodis.
Even if in the pictures he is kind and smiling, by nature he was hysterical.
"Zemgals with his sense of humiliation and emanence leaves exactly the opposite impression", says Auziņš.
Aivars Stranga, Professor of the Faculty of History and Philosophy of the University of Latvia in a conversation with "Delfi", agrees with Auziņa's assessment of Semigals as a calm and balanced statesman.
He also repossesss that Zemgals stood out with tribute to minorities, he regularly summoned Jewish representatives to the presidential palace.
"At that time, it wasn't customary, after Zemgal's presidency, Alberts Kviesis embraced this tradition", says the historian.
He was such a quiet and well-soaked man who had to persuad to become a president for a long time.
He didn't aspire for it, just didn't ligate fame.
He also viewed himself quite critically, because he did not know the French language, but believed that the president should definitely be able to do so.
"During the Presidency, the basics of the French language learned," Stranga explains.
Zemgals didn’t want to be persuaded again, he didn’t really like high positions at all.
In any case, he is a very respectable personality in the history of Latvia, already before getting to the presidential palace - he was a highly sought-after notary.
Zemgals also headed the famous Zemgals cashiers at Suvovorova iela 3, where there was an association of sacrificies and publishers, where the Latvian state was created practically in government negotiations.
He's always advanced.
There wasn't a big admirer of Ulmanis, but he didn't publicly object either.
"Frequent conversations have sometimes said that Ulmaṇa's actions do not sympathize," Stranga points out.
Having lived a peaceful life, there is no suspicion of illegality or corruption.
How does Zemgals be remembered today?
It's a tribute, but for a large part he is associated only with a street in Riga.
He's an absolute democrat, the views were centrist.
"There weren't any major turmoil at his time, namely, he could be associated with such a truly stable presidency," Stranga summed up.
During Gustavs Zemgal's presidency, from 1927 to 1930, a Latvian state visit to Sweden took place, which at that time implicitly implied full recognition in the neighboring country and its integration into the family of Northern European democracies.
On May 28, 1929, Zemgals made a state visit to Stockholm.
Reply of King Gustav V of Sweden took place on June 29, 1929.
The 2nd President of the Republic of Latvia was born in 1871 but passed away in January 1939.
Simple ways to help the child cope with attacks of anger.
I often have to deal with the fact that the angry of a child's angry, angry parents consider to be something wrong and insane.
When a child openly shows his anger - we don't don't know how to react, "Econet," says psychologist Jekaterina Kesa.
The specialist encourages a small conversation about these such natural and human feelings - anger and angnorance that everyone periodically experiences.
Aggression - this is already a physical activity directed against another person, and it usually appears when we feel angry.
When we, adults, are angry, excited - we usually know what's going to happen to us and how to deal with it.
We can express our anger and angry in ourselves: to express, to suppress, to hide, to keep in ourselves, to tell about it to our friends or other loved ones, we can in anger to kick a couch, smoke a cigarette (this is not the best way to dispile an anger!), take a shower, hit a cube around the table and do other things that we usually do not do.
We don't turn our aggression against others because we know different other ways to deal with these emotions.
Children, when angry, don’t understand what’s going to happen to them, how to name them and how to deal with them.
They can say, "Go away!", You are a fool", "Bad Mom", "Don't suffocate you", "Don't make friends with you" - and very rarely, when they are able to say "I get angry at you".
Children live in a literal sense, they are fully located right here, at this point, they are spontaneous and sincere in the performance of their feelings, and are also often captivated by various emotions.
It's very important that parents do not deny the child's feelings, including angry, don't shag about it and don't, but, on the contrary, help the child.
Is it necessary to pause in a relationship?
A long working day, in which everything hasn't gone the way you'd like, or just a bad mood - there's so many reasons you'd like you'd like for a moment, a day or even longer "dissacate" from all over the world.
Is it possible to pause a relationship for a while, too, and to breathe a little?
Does this also indicate that each of the partners has its own way to go?
Gentlemen of different ages divide into their opinions.
Jānis (23) is brief and concise: "In my opinion, if you need a pause in a relationship, don’t be with the right person".
Eduard (30), meanwhile, has unveiled a scheme that can possibly help someone.
If you feel that the partner begins to fall on your nerves for every small trifle, then surely - this is the right time to look at other hunting fields in order to understand if you miss him, or not especially think about him or not even want to see her anymore at all.
So the answer is - yes, withholding a pause can help to save time and understand how important the other half is for you.
Kaspars (29) reveals his theory.
In my opinion, it is necessary to keep a pause from each other in a daily relationship, because if you spend time with a person 24 hours a day, seven days a week, then quarrels are inevitable.
They are evoked by the fact that you notice every trifle that the co-wrote, and it begins to fall on the nerves.
People begin to get tired of each other, especially if there's nothing else to talk about, just what you've done during the day.
My personal experience makes me think so, so I haven't endured a relationship for more than a couple of months.
If at least for a moment you're thinking that it's better elsewhere or more - so why do this woman you've been together with deserves someone who can think that it isn't good enough here and there are paradise fields behind the mountains?
Thinking alone is already a little betrayal.
If there isn't, then there's none.
"I've got, that's why I don't condemn a little bit." says Edmund (46).
