Rozentāls with his enthusiascious and excited personality, Rozentāls first aroused the interest of the Latvian people on imaginative arts.
"In this sense, he was the first love of the Latvian people, which, as such, will remain unforgettable forever", Romans Suta once wrote about the old master of Latvian painting Janis Rozentāls.
Also in 2016, when it unveiled an old master's extensive retrospective exhibition in the restored main building of the Latvian National Museum of Art, it is clear that Rozentāls is the first love of Latvian art.
The exhibition was unveiled on August 12, but already from August 13 it is open to visitors and will be open until October 30.
The Latvian National Museum of Art honors the painter Janis Rozentāls (1866-1916) with an extensive retrospective in the newly opened Great Exhibition Hall, whose cent-fiftieth birthday on an international scale, is included in the UNESCO Celebration Calendar.
A retrospective exhibition featuring more than 150 works is one of Janis Rozental’s Anniversary Program’s central events.
A selection of works of art is mostly created from the collection of the Latvian National Museum of Art, which convincingly represents the main stages and genres of the classic creative activity, stylistic variability and variety of techniques.
Exhibits from Akseli Gallen-Kallella Museum, the Museum of Literature and Music, the Liepāja Museum, Tukums Museum, the Tukums Museum and a number of private collections in Latvia and Finland have also been used.
The ambiguity of the Century is revived by the presence of photographs, the mobile application of the exhibition reveals the convocations of individual works of art with photographs taken by the painter with his own camera.
The Finnish-Latvian Commonwealth Compatibility Society "Rozentāls-seura", which is named after a Latvian cathedral, at the Latvian National Museum of Art, at the Latvian National Museum of Art at the same time offers a small informative travelling exhibition "With a gaze in Finland, with a heart in Latvia".
A similar excavation with an outline of content in English, Finnish and Swedish will be opened in the "Virka" gallery in Helsinki in autumn.
