The Islamic State captured 2000 civilians in Syria.
Terrorist militia Islamic State militants fleeing Menbiza, in Aleppo's province of Syria, seized about 2000 civilians, used as a living shield, the US-sponsored Kurdish-Arab alliance and observers said on Friday.
"We didn't scell at them because they, fleeing to Jarābulus, took civilian with them," a spokeswoman for the Kurdu Menbiza's military council said in a statement.
After two-month-ending fighting, the Kurdish-guided Democracy Force (QSD), a U.S.-backed Kurd-Arab alliant alliance in northern Syria, announced on Friday that Menbiza has been liberated from jihadists.
The Islamic State Captured Menbiza in 2014.
The QSD already last week expelled the majority "Islamic State" militants from Menbiza, but a part held and showed fierce impossibility.
However, on Friday they abandoned the city, taking themselves captivities.
Islamists are going to Jarabulus, located near the Turkish border.
The London-based Syrian Observatory for Human Rights (SOHR) also reported that "Islam State" hijacked about 2000 people.
SOHR reported that Islamists hijacked the cars of the city's residents, forcibly seizing people in them, and then taken them to Jarabulus.
Islamic State militias have also made mass kidnappings in the past.
In January, Islamists conquered more than 400 civilian residents, including women and children, conquering territories in Deir ez Zore province in eastern Syria.
Later the Islamists freed about 270 of captivators.
How many Islamic State fighters have fled from Menbiza is unknown.
A spokeswoman for the Kurdu Menbiza's military council reported that the QSD managed to save 2500 people taken captivated by Islamists.
Information compiled by SOHR shows that at least 437 civilian residents, including 105 children, have lost their lives in the Battle of Menbiži.
299 QSD fighters and 1019 jihadists were killed.
Kolegova renounces candidacy for the position of Head of the SRS.
Inga Koļegova, the Head of the State Environmental Service, has decided to withdraw her candidacy for the position of the Director General of the State Revenue Service (SRS), LETA was informed by the Ministry of Finance.
Kolegova points out that discussing her candidacy has heightened controversy among the ruling coalition parties.
It seems that the true causes of the discussions are related to the visible, but more often, invisible interests of politicians and have nothing to do with the assessment of my professional abilities.
"That's not acceptable to me either," emphasizes now the candidate for the position of the Head of the SRS.
If her candidacy was confirmed by the government, it is clear that it would not be a convincing vote and it would not allow him to fully work and realize the necessary changes to the SRS, feeling the support of the entire government.
Without it, full-fledged work would not be possible, Koļegova concludes.
"Multi-on-on-time I've publicly emphasized that I don't want to be politically addicted, but at the moment the whole process of my candidacy is too politicized," explains the official.
"Thank you for my trust and support, it's an experience that I evaluate as a benefit," Kolegova writes in the application Reizniece-Ozola.
Unity expressed on Wednesday that during the meeting with Kolegova revealed "substantial contradictions" between the candidate's explanations and the information available in the public registers about SIA Pallogs divination costs, because the publicly available decisions of Pallogs members' meetings provide for non-distributing profits.
Dace Pelēkā, Deputy Director-General of the SRS is currently acting as the Head of the SRS. Dace Pelēkā.
Līvāni police are involved in rescuing two unattended children, the Latgale Regional Administration of the State Police informs.
News about unattended strollers with infants was received on Friday around 7 p.m.
They were located in Līvāni next to the former store building.
The two little girls were immediately handed to the hands of the medics to provide assistance.
A purse with documents and a mobile phone was also left in the trolley.
In less than half an hour, the law enforcement officers found out the possible location of the children's occupiers and went there.
Arriving at the woman's civil husband's home, police officers found out that the woman was sleeping because she is heavily under the influence of alcohol.
According to preliminary information, a woman, who was Born in 1992, visited a friend where she used alcohol.
Already going home after sitting together, the woman has decided to leave the children at the store under undetermined conditions.
The police immediately informed the Orphan's and Custody Court about what happened, which will also decide on the further fate of the children.
The Freeport of Riga has introduced all the recommendations of the State Audit Office with regard to the Krievu Island project.
The Freeport of Riga Authority has implemented all out of a total of five State Audit Office (SEC) recommendations regarding the development of the project Infraturgation on the Krievu Island for the re-election of port activities from the city centre, Diena.lv was informed by Diena.lv.
Approval of the project is planned at the nearest meeting of the Freeport of Riga Board.
In order for the recommendations to be implemented in accordance with the opinion of the SAO, preparing the documentation necessary for the implementation of the recommendations, the Freeport of Riga Authority was also guided by the conclusions of the legal and operational audit carried out by the International Auditor General Deloitte at the Freeport of Riga Authority and the submitted recommendations for the improvement of project management in the later stage of the project.
Information on the implementation of the recommendations has also been sent to the State Audit Office today.
Regardless that the Freeport of Riga Authority does not agree with the opinion prepared by the State Audit Office on the expediency for the implementation of the Krievu Island project, we treat the implementation of the project very responsibly and the European Commission (EC).
I would like to emphasise that all construction works planned within the framework of the project have been fully completed within the set deadlines and in the planned amount.
"In order to prove to all those involved involved in the implementation of the project, including the EC, the SAO and the stevedores, that the main objective of the Freeport is to successfully carry out the project, we have fully implemented all the recommendations of the SAO in relation to project management and monitoring issues," says Sanita Kaire, project manager of Krievu Island.
The existing long-term action plan to achieve the objective of the project was supplemented with the most important planned activities of the involved parties and the deadlines of the necessary decisions, definitions of meetings and regular reports and their deadlines, planned construction process monitoring tools, as well as the definitions of the most important boundary points.
Until 31 December 2018, the approved action plan to achieve the objective of the project has been supplemented with performance monitoring activities in accordance with the recommendations of the SAO, thus reinforcing the control of the deadlines for the activities of other parties involved in the implementation of the project by the Freeport of Riga.
A project management plan has been re-established to improve project management and reduce potential risks, defining project management areas and indicating the developed documents that provide a control and monitoring mechanism for each of them.
To increase the capacity of the project, there has been a project management structural scheme, which reflects the planned structure of the project management team, the qualification and functions of the employees.
In order to improve the communication of the Freeport of Riga Authority with the parties involved in the project, a communication plan has been drawn up in the project, which, in addition to the parties set out in the action plan of the project, shall define the stakeholders for the successful completion of the project, information exchange principles, types and periodicity.
In turn, in order to improve the control of the use of financial resources, it will be necessary to substantiate in more detail the necessity of entering into a service contract or the commencement of procurement, and the rule of law.
Within the framework of the project, a port infrastructure conforming to world standards has been built on Krievu Island - four new deep-water berths, railway accessories, as well as engineering communications.
Structures co-financed by the Cohesion Fund have been completed and put into operation at the end of the last year, achieving EUR 10.6 million in the planned financing.
As a result of the Threats in Slovakia increased security at airports.
After a non-specificial threat, Slovakia stepped up security measures at several airports on Friday, the Ministry of the Interior has announced.
"Given the threat, we have stepped up security measures at international airports in Bratisław, Poprad and Košice," a spokeswoman for the Slovak Ministry of the Interior told AFP on Friday.
"National Safety and Analysis Center is currently analyzing the information we've received," he commented.
An informal European Union (EU) summit will take place in the Slovak capital on 16 September, which will be attended by all the bloc's countries except Great Britain.
The meeting will discuss the consequences of the British Brexit referendum and discussed the future of the EU without Britain.
Slovakia until January holds the Presidency of the EU.
German researchers are discussing the number of "Berlin Wall Victims".
According to a new study, the number of fatalities near a communist yoke the former East German border could be much higher than previously believed, but doubts about the true number of casualties have still not been dispelled.
According to data released on Wednesday by the Checkpoint Charlie Museum of the Berlin Wall Museum between 1945-1945 and 1989 at the border with western allied zones and the later Western German Communist authorities murdered 1841 people who have attempted to get out of their controlled territory.
The museum has been studying border-associated deaths for a long time for years and occasionally rectifies the number of casualties.
Earlier this year, 11 more deaths have been added to the list, including a six-year-year-old boy who drowned a family trying to get to West Germany through then-Chechoslovakia and Hungary.
Among the vicious victims of the Berlin Wall there are also defectors of the Soviet army.
"The final extent of the damage caused by the dual evils of communism and socialism has not yet been fully documented", admits Aleksandra Hildebrante, the director of the museum.
"Our task is to ensure that the graves of those who perpetrators who died trying to flee from a communist-controlled country are groomed and do not disappear," Hildebrant said in a statement circulated.
However, other researchers have already questioned the new figures.
"Number 1841 is unconvinced," Johen Statt, An expert in the history of the Free University of the Free University of Berlin, said in an interview with Süddeutsche Zeitung.
He, along with other researchers from the Free University of Berlin, is currently reviewing possible suspicious deaths at the border, and so far they have managed to valid only 2,60.
In the past, the researchers estimated that the communists murdered 138 people only near the Berlin Wall.
In view of those people who have perished in an effort to escape the Baltic Sea or get to the west through the territory of neighboring countries, the number of fatalities could reach about a thousand.
However, this is still significantly less than 1841.
One of the reasons for the different number of casualties could be different methodologies.
Checkpoint Charlie's museum also accounts for all suicides of East German border guards, whether or not they were directly related to their service.
The museum also counts deaths unrelated to direct violism.
"If seniors between the ages of 70 and 80 after shrinking with border controls died of cardiac strike, it should have the consequences of this "lander border," Hildebrante believes.
However, Stat is convinced that a distinction should be made between the old people who may have died as a result of the treatment they have witnessed, and those whose death has been caused by long-awaiting in hot weather on a bus at the border.
Irrespective of it, accounting for the victims is not easy, because the Communist Eastern German authorities did not accrue such statistical data, and the researchers should examine the documents in various scattered archives, says Statt.
However, the State's group has recently issued new documents and could therefore come up with an estimate of the number of casualties in the near future.
