Greenland shark is the longest-living vert vertebrate - video review
Scientists argue that Greenland's sharks have the longest lifespan of all vertebrates on our planet.
Shark explorer Julius Nielsen says a long-living record belongs to a womb, which could be 272 to 512 years old and five-metre long.
A French court dismisses a claim to demolish shops in the Jungle refugee camp.
A French court dismissed Kale's request to demolish dozens of self-creatended shops and restaurants in the jungle camp where several thousand refugees live.
At a hearing this week in Kaleu, the authorities indicated that the state should demolish 72 self-created breeches, where people sell a wide variety of goods in the camp, such as cigarettes and energy drinks, offer hairdresser's services, tea for 50 cents (40p) and simple meals for a few euro self-creatended restaurants.
Kale Prefect office pointed out that these self-created companies have to be demolished, because they form a shadow economy and do not pay taxes, as well as create fire and sanitation risks.
Representatives of the Humoon Group explained to the court that these small businesses are essential, as food provided by the government and support groups does not meet the needs of the camp, in which the number of occupiers continues to rise.
They also pointed out that self-created boutiques and restaurants often offer complimentary shelter and food for those who need it most.
Lille's court ruled that there is no legal basis to expel people who have created 72 self-created breeches, but it realises Kale prefect worries.
Judge Jean-French Molla said food, coffee and restaurants play a bigger role than simply providing food for people who live in "extremely humiliating conditions."
He pointed out that these venues are peaceful meeting places for refugees and volunteers.
One place at risk of demolition is the Jungle Book Kids camp cafe, which provides more than 200 free meals a day, English-frean language classes and provide consultations on asylum issues for hundreds of unaccompanied non-grown minors unaccompanied.
More than 170 000 people have signed a petition for the protection of a children's cafe.
A letter addressed to Guardian states that liquidation of the cafe "what would be a catastrophe for these children, some of whom are only 8 years old."
On Friday, two charritable organizations "Help for Refugees" and "L'Auberge des Migrants" reported that the number of occupiers of "Jungle" camp has risen to 9000 people, which is the highest figure since the rapid influx of migrants in the summer.
Britain wins silver in the rugby-7 series fight, when Fiji's national team masterfully wins gold.
Britain's suffocation in the Rugby-7 Series stage ended on Thursday, when Rio Fiji was convincingly shattered by a score of 43:7.
Fiji demonstrated a masterclass in the management of the ball, playing balls with a leg, a support game and a brutal power game, winning the gold - the first Olympic medal in the history of its existence.
Defeat brought a reputable consolation prize - a silver medal, which that day was Britain's third medal. The British national team can be proud of its success in the first Olympic rugby-7 tournament, in which they beat New Zealand, Argentina and South Africa.
A heavy fight in the semi-finals, in which Bock's team suffered a beaten with a score of 7:5 in the first half of the day, allegedly reached Britain's final.
But in any case, the Fiji team is currently in its best form to defeat the 15-man team as well.
Although unsuccessful nonetheless, some confusing for Britain could be the fact that at least one Briton won a gold medal. This is Fiji national team head coach Ben Ryan - a former English coach from Wimbledon.
For Great Britain, it was a thrilling adventurous adventure.
Eight years ago, under Ryan's lead, they lost all of the games in the rugby-7 series stage in Adelaide.
This time they lost only one game.
Defeat is painful, but growth is astonishing.
Fiji spectacularly embossed the match, replenishing Britain's power techniques for the sake of joy.
For captain Thomas Mitchell and comrades, it was like collaborating with several oncoming buses, and World Series champions took the lead with 5:0, when Osea Kolinisau scored the first touchdown.
Jerry Tuwai also increased the lead even more in the last four minutes, realizing a touchdown in the right wrench.
Score at 12:0 and the British team didn't even really hit the ball.
Michelle's excellent power technique prevented the beaten Leon Nakarava from scoring a three-point, but that only slightly influenced Fiji's score.
In the seventh minute of the game, Jasa Veremalua scored a touch-down in the right-hand, securing the lead over Britain to 17:00.
Nakarava further increased the lead after the rematch and had more than a minute left until the end... the end of the first half.
Vatemo Ravouvou shivered some awkward British force techniques and crossed the line, scoring points.
The landing secured a score of 29:0.
It seemed that Fiji were born to play rugby-7 matches, while Britons were still learning.
Actually, it's true.
The British team made a prominent change in halftime, breaking Fiji's player line... a full four-strengthening four-strength, which, according to first-half-time standards, until Joshua Tuisova scored a few more points for Fiji's team-same.
Dan Norton managed to scourge in favour of the British team, making a touchdown in the left corner and scoring at 36:7, but the game had already been lost, meanwhile fans of South Sea islanders sang, ventilated the flags and dancing in the tribunes.
You can’t admire Fiji’ cheerful attitude.
Mata Viliame made Fiji's victory even sweeter with another touchdown at the end of the game.
After the final whistle of the game, Fiji could start celebrating, but the Britons were able to sit in the grass after the defeat.
In the first half-final of the day Fiji defeated Japan with a score of 20:5, while the Brits were superior to South Africa, where in a very fierce battle the two teams scored a touchdown and the South fricans lost by only a few points.
Kyle Braun put Box in the lead in the first half, but in the second half, Dan Norton crossed the line with a shiky skill and took the ball between the poles, securing a victory for the Britons.
Marcus Watsson, who's English rugby-15 team player Anthonia's breeze, an excellent power effort at the end of the game secured a victory and another silver medal for the team.
Coach's fight with cancer inspired Helen Glower and Heter Staning to win the rowing competition.
It's going to be the most fevitorious gold medal in these races and it's already expressing a lot.
From the first to the last swipe, Helena Glover and Hetere Stasing had a special meaning on Estacio da Lagoa's track.
Although there are only both athletes in the boat, they were accompanied by an echo of Robin Williams, who inspired them until the second Olympic gold medal in rowing women, becoming the first women in the history of British sport to have made such a success.
They unequivocally prevailed, because for the 39th time they got an unbeatured, unprecedented victory.
The two great athletes gliding along the surface of the water without fully reflecting this triumphant with the elegance of the swan.
Behind every gold medwk in rowing stands a whole team that is working hard for athletes to continuously improve their performance and the biggest contribution to their success was made by the coach, who still 30 months ago did not know if he experienced this day because he was diagnosed with cancer.
According to him, it disappeared as quickly as it emerged.
I've had bladder cancer, which's one that can be operated on, and it also happened.
If you don't, it would spread all over the body.
And the situation becomes uncontrollable.
It's changing your vision a little bit.
For William, cancer was unveiled in December 2013.
This trio was fortunate that William was able to start the recovery procedure at the start of the 2014 mid-season and to reduce the duration of the break to a minimum if it can be said.
I needed an income, that's why I had to regain it quickly.
It stimulated the recovery.
I was in the hospital and I walked 6, 10 and even 15-metre-long distances, even though the doctor said there would suffice a few steps.
That's how it all started.
Rowing in itself is a challenge, but this time it was even bigger.
After the victory, the first thought at once was about Williams, who was very upset all the morning and talked more than usual under the influence of excitement.
Stanning, who has served in the Royal Arthylenerian unit of Afghanistan, said: "This was an emotionally difficult week, but it means a lot to me."
It only confirms how much it means to us and how much I, Helen and Robin we've worked.
With Helen and Robin, 3 years have been great.
Without Robin, we wouldn't have achieved anything, so a huge thank you to him, he's the best trainer in the world.
I couldn't conceive a better training mate or a better trainer.
Helēna Glover and Heter Staning haven't lost any races in five years.
The pair reach such tremendous speed with which competitors can't compete.
Even in the last 500 metropolises, when teams from New Zealand and Denmark began to get closer, Staning and Glover's predominance was too great.
We felt every moment.
Helen shouted: "Pertains the rhythm, maintains the griom."
Don't lose concentration is really important.
We managed a good start, but at the same time we didn’t have high expectations.
That day we didn't have the intention to lying the records, but just rowing well and winning the race, and that's what we didn't.
For a couple of years will be rested to decide what to do next.
In the next month there will be the wedding of Glover and television adventurer Steve Bashall.
For the time being, she just wants to enjoy the moment.
I didn't want to say that this was my last race.
That would have been too much pressure.
In London, it took me about six months to realise I'm an olympic champion.
It was a new experience and I was very excited.
This time it took about four minutes.
She said, "Sensation is fantastic."
