Depressive food during the depressive period is "Dueful Diet"
In March 1933, shortly after the election as president, Franklin Delano Roosevelt sat in an oval room to have a meal.
President Rūzvelts tasted fine culinary delicacies such as goose-to-fashion pamper or fura and Maryland turtle soup.
But that's day his meal was much more modest: stuffed eggs in a tomato sauce, potato porridge and pluture pudding for dessert.
Jane Ziegelmann says: "It's a way to show solidarity in cooking with people who were very hard."
Her husband Andrew Kou adds: "That's also a message for the Americans, how should be eaten."
A couple from the Brooklyn Heights district are food historians.
The Kou last book "Vegetable Sautjums" describes Chinese cuisine in America, while Ziegelmane describes life in her book "97 Apple Trees" through food in a rental house at Manhattan's eastern break.
Their new joint work "Satuary Dalit," published by Harper on Tuesday is the story of American food during the Great Destroy.
Showcasing some solidarity in the culinary, they withheld a meal together with the reporters "Eismenberg's Sandwich Shop," in a simple dining eatery, located in the area of Flatiron and running from 1929, when the Great Depression began.
The 54-year-old Mrs Ziegelmane commissioned a sandwich with smelted make-cooked olives, while 57-year-old Kou ordered turkey, potato porridge and vegetable lettuce.
When a reporter ordered a fake harbour, the couple admitted that his choice is very appropriate for a conversation about Great Depression Time Food.
Ms Ziegelmane told that the fake hare was very popular.
There was a peanut harbour, liver harbour and bean hare.
They were cooked from the basic ingredients and other ingredients that enriched the meal.
Imagine what it's like to eat so many peanuts that they're at lunch.
Mr. Kou escaped.
"Surely it seemed that the belly was fraught with lead," he added.
In the pre-depression, the American dining table resembled a Swedish table, especially in rural areas.
Women were preparing to eat for their men-workers and for breakfast, freshly cooked casseroles were served at lunch and dinner.
During the First World War, it was the Americans who sent food to the Europeans. "Satuary Diet" describes how the Americans adapted to the circumstances, when Amerika suddenly ceased to have an abbrevillation land.
"At that time, the food became an object of attention of the American people on a day-to-day basis, "said Mr. Kou, explaining why he and his wife wanted to write on this topic.
Equally significant became the new budget diets: unknown dishes such as fake hares and stews became a day-to-to-day, stews were an "excellent way to use leftovers," Ms. Ziegelmann noted, as the chefs were able to hide the unattractive ingredients under a creamy sauce.
Fresh fruits were replaced by inexpensive dried fruits.
Meat, which for years was the main components of American meals, now became an extremely rare luxury commodity.
At a time when the number of calories and the nutritional value of the food was more important than the taste, new, unseen formations were born.
In an elongated cookbook, Mrs. Ziegelmane found a recipe for how to make onions filled with pea-tree butter.
Mr Kou adds: "It wasn't a popular addition to the dining table."
Mrs Ziegelman said: "It was surreal."
Peanut Butter and fried onion are not compatible.
Many recipes of homemade dishes adapted to difficult times.
Household economists such as Louise Stanley, Head of the Federal Bureau of Home Economics from 1923 to 1943, determined the country's eating habits, which had not been experienced either before the Great Depression or after it.
By posting articles and recipes in newspapers and magazines, they advised housewives to become "economists" and embrace such challenges as turning a misk of macaroni and carrot in a tasty meal.
"Foodful Meal" is a feast of historical delicacies.
Evidence about the government's attitude towards his hungry citizens is particularly interesting, such as President Herbert Houver "allow me to Eat Cake."
Even though he publicly talked about his sleepth childhood and catering in Iowa, in fact he ate as a subtle millionaire he had become.
Mr. Kou says: "She liked European cuisine, such as a fish with cucumber sauce."
He ate an almost gild-plated living room, wearing a lunch blazer.
He's completely detached out of reality.
President Roosevelt would have made the same a mistake if his wife Eleonora hadn't advised him to set an example with his eating habits.
The book tells that Ruzvelt has hired a housemator who did not know how to cook, so the White House became a place "whelmed not one of the most unscrupulous dishes in Washington, but also the worst-co-cooked dishes".
As soon as the country's economy was recovering, most of the culinary paratas of the depressive era disappeared.
But we continue to pay attention to the calorie and observance, as well as on how to apply science in culinary, Mrs Ziegelmann added.
The couple says that when they wrote the book, they gained a greater understanding of Ziegelmane's mother's eating habits, for example, why she couldn't thrive food.
"She was genuinely afraid of throwing the food out," Mr Kou said.
Ms Ziegelman said, "She actually travested people."
Once there was an incident with wind cakes.
Having eaten at Eysenberg's Eisenberg's eater, a pair of writers pulled a dessert made from the bag at home: plum pudding that President Roosevelt tended to eat for lunch.
"It's plum whipping," Mrs Ziegelman said, adding that at that time there was a common crave habit for modest dishes to give a chic name.
It does not contain whipped cream, only dried plums, slightly flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
Everyone impatiently soaked spucoops in the brown-smash, which was on plates.
Surprisingly, it wasn't that bad.
Mr. Kou ate a few more morsels.
Sounding after an experienced "Economan", he said to his wife: "A pinch of ground hazelnut."
