The reason for pick-up of the code even more enraged the film director.
"Most of the guests are arabic and they demand an eavesdropping of an Israeli code," Lanzman cites an explanation from an apprehension.
Suddeutsche Zeitung reports that Israel's ambassador to Germany Jakov Hadas-Handelsman allegedly called the case "great shame."
We're impressed and shocked by this incident.
It's great shame in itself.
And the fact that it has happened right in Germany, there is an even greater humiliation in this network of hotels.
Explanations are not necessary.
"We hope the hotel will draw the right conclusions".
This event also gained a fairly wide resonance on the Internet, where people dubbed it a scandal.
Others believe Kempinski's hotel has simply indulged Arab customers.
Someone else's post "ashamed," pointing out that Kurfurstendam is located on Kurfurstendam street next to the synagogue.
Der Spiegel reported that the hotel itself quickly rejected all reproaches, announishing that it was an "misunderstanding" and apologized to Lanzman.
"We haven't received a direct injunction to remove Israel from the list of national numbers," Suddeutsche Zeitung cites Kempinski's representative.
And it adds that the code is again added to the list.
Police are using rubber bullets and tear gas against protesting Brazilian students.
A crunch between students and police took place on Thursday in São Paulo's city of Braziland, where students gathered for a defensive respecify the reforms of the government's education system.
The police used rubber bullets and tear gas against the students.
Trouble began when ombudsmans attempted to excrete students, as a result of which police used rubber bullets and tear gas.
