Britain has to maintain an Olympic spirit and extend Heathrow.
Heathrow will not only contribute to prosperity and growth in local regions and in London, but also throughout the country.
This, in my opinion, makes Heathrow very attractive, just like in 2012, where we invested tremendous work so that all regions of the country would benefit from the Olympic Games.
Stratford and Eastend got a tremendously a lot from the Olympics.
And we're going to get it done once again by providing local residents and businesses with business opportunities near Heathrow.
For local communities alone, we're going to create 10 000 new business opportunities, 40 000 new jobs, and finally put an end to youth unemployment around Heathrow.
Just like at the London Olympic Games in 2012, there will be people this time who will believe that this challenge is too great and that the expansion of Heathrow is too ambitious.
At the time, people said Britain would never be able to prearrterm at the time of the Olympics and that it would be a global failure.
Sceptics made a mistake about 2012 and also in the case of Heathrow they won't be right.
There's always an hurdles that need to be overcome, but Heathrow has so far overcame everything.
Just like the Olympic Games, Heathrow is an infrastructure project that will benefit the whole society, rather than individual individuals.
Enlargement is an opportunity to generate up to 180 000 new jobs, 211-bill economic growth, creating a breeding ground for both employment and experience at the airport and across the United Kingdom.
Heathrow operates a great team that has created an 11-billion-buy-dollar infrastructure investment program, within a period of time and in the budget starting with Terminal 5, which for five years has been dubbed the best airport terminal in the world to Terminal 2, which has been built between the two ever-exploited runways in the world.
Now they are ready to set up a third run-up route, which will be financed by private investments of 16 billion pounds.
The 2012 London Olympics was a great success, and I'm sure Heathrow's expansion will as well.
It's time to revive the Olympic spirit and expand Heathrow by safeguarding the future of our trade and creating a new employment environment that Britain could be proud of.
Lord Paul Dayton is a member of the Board of Heathrow and former Commercial Secretary of the Ministry of Economics.
In Switzerland, a woman runs on the highway, trying to commit suicide.
In the middle of the past night, he slept on the highway.
Police are attempting to find witnesses to an accident when several cars crossed over several cars early in the morning on the highway.
The incident took place Thursday shortly after mid-a-follow on the A1 road near Morges's City of Vo Canton.
A vo cannton's police report states that deceiving suicide deceiving, the woman had slept on the road in the dark, where several cars drove him.
Unequivocally trying to kill himself, a victim, a 49-year-old she-woman, drove several cars, including a truck.
She died at the scene.
A number of drivers involved in the accident have already provided testimonies, while others have not yet been identified, say the police, who are appealing the drivers involved and witnesses to the accident.
A criminal enquiry has also begun in the case, a logical step in such a case, lawyer Gilles Hoffstler told Le Tribune de Geneve.
However, it is not yet known whether any of the drivers involved will be punished.
Was it possible to avoid a collision?
Hofsteller adds: "That will be clarified by the prosecutor."
Two similar cases that have taken place in the last 5 years indicate that none of the drivers will be brought to justice.
In both cases, the pedestrian in the middle of the night had gone out on the highway, so it was decided that drivers could not predict that in the middle of the night there would be a pedestrian on the highway, says the newspaper.
Last year, a colist was acquivocally pleasing for negligence when, in August 2014, she drove a man who lying in the middle of the night on an A1 road near Payärne.
The court clarified that the 22-year-old victim had drunk and later noticed falling asleep on the road.
The court concluded that the co-president, who was on his way to the scene of the accident, drove in an appropriate way, and the referee admitted that the lying man on the road was an "complete unpredictable and exceptional case."
Anyone who has information about the accident on Thursday near Morges has been urged to contact the police by calling 021 644 83 46 or 021 644 44 44.
Signatures are collected on the Internet for organising a new SRS manager's job competition.
On the study portal "Avaaz.org", the collection of signatures has been commenced to organize a new competition for the position of the Head of the State Revenue Service (SRS).
A petition addressed to the ministers of the National Alliance (NA) and the Union of Greens and Farmers (ZZS) states that the society wants a transparent and professional process of nominating officials.
The petition has been established on August 10 and has now been signed by 55 people.
Inga Koļegova, a candidate for the position of SRS director-general, is opaque and non-professional.
It evokes in the memory of the best Soviet functionary traditions.
It discredits public administration and once again makes the public disapprove in government and politicians.
"There is a need for a concrefiguration with clear criteria against which all the candidates should be judged, the text of the petition says.
Its author Jānis B. pointed out that there is no knowledge of the criteria chosen by Kolegova for the position chosen by Kolegova.
It is also not clear what is the candidate's plans to work at the SRS.
It is indicated in the petition that the State Chancellery should create a new, open competition, in which professional candidates are actively addressed, recruitment specialists are attracted and an appropriate salary is offered.
Finance Minister Dana Reizniece-Ozola last week officially nominate Koļegova for the position of the head of the SRS, this week it was planned to confirm her in the new position in the government, but all the ministers of the party "Unity" voted against, so the question of a week was postponed.
