Conservation producer "Zilā lagūna" turnover last year shrank by 33.8%.
The turnover of the Canning producer "Zilā lagūna" last year amounted to 4.202 million euros, which is 33.8% less than a year ago, while the company's losses fell 5.7 times - to 904.7 thousand euros, according to the annual financial report published in the database "Lursoft".
The company's management report states that, in 2015, the company posted losses due to a number of reasons, inter alia, due to a constant lack of working capital, which was triggered by "Unicredit Bank" leaving the Baltic market, terminating the credit line previously in force, and leaving "Zilo lagūna" with concluded contracts without being able to execute them.
As a result, the company did not have sufficient funds to buy cheaper raw materials in large volumes and receive a volume discount.
Leaving the relevant bank from the Latvian market also prevented the company from enforcing European Union Structural Funds support projects, which would have allowed the production unit to purchase new production equipment and modernise the infrastructure.
Likewise, the company's financial indicators in the last year were negatively influenced by the rise in the prices of raw materials and the unwillingness of customers to pay more for the goods produced by the company.
"Only in the second half of July 2016, customers recognized price changes and were willing to buy goods at the new - the highest prices", the company's management points out.
In 2015, "Zilā lagūna" also started discussions with potential investors, including the valuation of the company's movable and immovable property.
Investigators have already been with the company's largest creditor "Unicredit Leasing", where the parties have got to know and discussed possible investment plans.
At the same time, the operation and development of the company was positively influenced by the implementation of the restructuring and optimization plan started in 2014 and 2015.
This plan included improving and optimization of technological processes, which allows to achieve greater production productivity and capacity.
"Several equipment was purchased that provides a higher production rate," the company's management said.
"Zila lagūna" management notes that the purchased equipment in 2016 will allow the company's production unit to fulfill such orders, which until now was not possible.
The first batch of production, which will be produced on this equipment, will be ready in August.
Since intent protocols have already been signed regarding the sale of the product, all production will be marketed in accordance with indemnity protocols.
The new product will increase the turnover by about 300 thousand euros per month", the company's management points out.
In August 2016, the company also plans to sign a co-operating cooperation agreement on supplies of fish products to Iraq.
"Blue Lagoon" in 2016 sent the first experimental container to Israel, expanded the sales market to Cyprus and will sign a co-operation agreement with a new supermarket chain in the U.S. in September this year.
As a result of all the above-mentioned measures, the company plans to increase its turnover.
In 2014, "Zilā lagūna" had a turnover of 6.347 million euros and a loss of 5.113 million euros.
"Blue Lagūna" was founded in 1997 and its share capital is 1.156 million euros.
The company belonged to two Latvian individuals - Mikhail Kaidanov (89,11%) and Igor Shirhon (10.89%).
